WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.242
Picture Maya, the only AI architect at an insurer, with three requests on Monday.

2
00:00:05.572 --> 00:00:10.151
Automate claims, brief strategy on competitors, and add a website chat assistant.

3
00:00:10.951 --> 00:00:13.931
A vendor pitches one autonomous agent for all three.

4
00:00:14.261 --> 00:00:19.011
Maya runs a five-step decision procedure instead, and gets three different answers.

5
00:00:19.811 --> 00:00:22.321
Step one: extract the hard constraints first.

6
00:00:22.651 --> 00:00:25.503
Latency, cost, data residency, auditability,

7
00:00:25.553 --> 00:00:28.339
and accuracy floor eliminate options early.

8
00:00:29.139 --> 00:00:31.863
Step two: is the sequence of steps known in advance?

9
00:00:32.193 --> 00:00:36.452
Yes means a pipeline or Plan and Execute; no means a tool-calling loop.

10
00:00:37.252 --> 00:00:40.679
Step three: are sub-tasks independent enough to run in parallel?

11
00:00:41.009 --> 00:00:46.250
Step four: do privileges, context, or models need isolating into separate agents?

12
00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.264
Step five: name the termination and escalation policy.

13
00:00:50.594 --> 00:00:54.939
Without a stop condition and a human handoff rule, the design is incomplete.

14
00:00:55.739 --> 00:01:00.105
Claims follow fixed, regulated steps, fifty thousand a day, all auditable.

15
00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:05.825
So: a deterministic pipeline with LLM components inside the stages, not an agent.

16
00:01:06.625 --> 00:01:10.457
Every stage is versioned, evaluated, and written to an audit trail.

17
00:01:10.787 --> 00:01:14.257
Low-confidence fraud flags escalate to a human adjuster.

18
00:01:15.057 --> 00:01:18.507
The competitor brief splits into four independent threads:

19
00:01:18.557 --> 00:01:20.981
funding, launches, hiring, and sentiment.

20
00:01:21.311 --> 00:01:25.613
Ten minutes is fine, so a supervisor runs four research workers in parallel.

21
00:01:26.413 --> 00:01:30.544
Each worker has its own context window and returns a cited summary.

22
00:01:30.874 --> 00:01:35.368
A single ReAct agent would work one thread at a time and drown in context.

23
00:01:36.168 --> 00:01:40.257
The chat widget must answer in under two seconds, at five hundred sessions.

24
00:01:40.587 --> 00:01:44.249
Loops won't fit, so it's RAG with a router and a confidence gate.

25
00:01:45.049 --> 00:01:48.988
When grounding looks weak, it hands off to a human with the full conversation.

26
00:01:49.318 --> 00:01:53.065
Prefix caching on the system prompt keeps time to first token low.

27
00:01:53.865 --> 00:01:57.383
A full design also covers state, failure containment,

28
00:01:57.433 --> 00:01:59.874
tracing, and a golden test set in CI.

29
00:02:00.204 --> 00:02:02.650
And it shows exactly where a human sits in the loop.

30
00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:05.972
On the exam, sub-second means no loops,

31
00:02:06.022 --> 00:02:09.203
and regulated means deterministic, logged stages.

32
00:02:09.533 --> 00:02:12.448
Happy-path-only answers are usually distractors,

33
00:02:12.498 --> 00:02:14.795
so read on and practise the procedure.
